## Plan archetypes

Different change types take different plan shapes. Pick the archetype
before writing the plan.

### Archetype 1 — Additive feature (the default)

A new feature touching multiple modules. Stages flow bottom-up through
the hierarchy. Each stage produces a buildable intermediate state.

```markdown
# Plan: add Order CRUD feature

## Stage 1 — Model
- Add `Order.java` (Immutables interface) + `OrderRecord.java` (JDBC record)
- Add `OrderRequest`, `OrderResponse` DTOs
**Validate:** `mvn -pl Model install`

{{- if .HasModule "SQLDatastore"}}

## Stage 2 — SQLDatastore
- Add `OrderRepository extends CrudRepository<OrderRecord, Long>`
- Add `V2__create_orders.sql` migration
**Validate:** `mvn -pl SQLDatastore -am test`
**Depends on:** Stage 1 (Order, OrderRecord types)
{{- end}}

{{- if .HasModule "Shared"}}

## Stage 3 — Shared
- Add `OrderService` with constructor-injected `OrderRepository`
- Add `OrderServiceTest` (unit, MockitoExtension)
**Validate:** `mvn -pl Shared -am test`
**Depends on:** Stage 2 (OrderRepository)
{{- end}}

{{- if .HasModule "API"}}

## Stage 4 — API
- Add `OrderController` with `@PostMapping`, `@GetMapping("/{id}")`, etc.
- Add `OrderControllerIT` (integration, against Testcontainers)
**Validate:** `mvn -pl API -am verify`
**Depends on:** Stage 3 (OrderService)
{{- end}}

## Final verification
- [ ] `mvn install` succeeds across all modules
- [ ] `trabuco doctor` reports clean
```

### Archetype 2 — Job feature

Special shape because of the **contract/consumer separation**. Job
contracts live in `Jobs` (a contract module); the executor lives in
`Worker`. Services that ENQUEUE jobs depend on `Jobs` only — not Worker.

{{- if and (.HasModule "Jobs") (.HasModule "Worker")}}

```markdown
# Plan: add ProcessShipment background job

## Stage 1 — Model: no changes (using existing types)

## Stage 2 — Jobs
- Add `ProcessShipmentJobRequest` (record implementing JobRequest)
- Add `ProcessShipmentJobRequestHandler` (no-op base class)
**Validate:** `mvn -pl Jobs -am install`
**Depends on:** Stage 1 entities (if payload references domain types)

{{- if .HasModule "Shared"}}

## Stage 4 — Shared
- Update `OrderService.placeOrder` to enqueue:
  `BackgroundJobRequest.enqueue(new ProcessShipmentJobRequest(order.id()))`
- Note: Shared depends on Jobs (contract), NOT Worker (executor) —
  this is the loose-coupling payoff of the separate Jobs module
**Validate:** `mvn -pl Shared -am test`
**Depends on:** Stage 2 (JobRequest type)
{{- end}}

## Stage 5 — Worker
- Add concrete `@Component ProcessShipmentJobRequestHandler` extending
  the abstract handler from Jobs
- Replace `throw new UnsupportedOperationException` with real logic
**Validate:** `mvn -pl Worker -am verify`
**Depends on:** Stage 2 (abstract handler base)

## Final verification
- [ ] `mvn install`
- [ ] Manually enqueue a job and observe the handler executing
```
{{- else}}

(This project does not include the Worker module — job features are
not applicable. Add the Worker module via `trabuco add Worker` if you
need background processing.)
{{- end}}

### Archetype 3 — Event feature

Same shape as Job features: contract/consumer separation between
`Events` (contract module + EventPublisher) and `EventConsumer` (the
listener). Services that PUBLISH events depend only on `Events`.

{{- if and (.HasModule "Events") (.HasModule "EventConsumer")}}

```markdown
# Plan: add OrderShipped domain event

## Stage 1 — Model: no changes

## Stage 2 — Events
- Add `OrderShipped` record to the sealed event hierarchy
- Update parent's `permits` clause to include `OrderShipped`
- Note: this is one of the rare ATOMIC edits within stratified planning —
  the record + permits clause must change together; partial state breaks
  the seal
**Validate:** `mvn -pl Events -am install`

{{- if .HasModule "Shared"}}

## Stage 4 — Shared
- Update `OrderService.shipOrder` to call `eventPublisher.publish(new OrderShipped(...))`
- Shared depends on Events (contract), NOT EventConsumer (listener)
**Validate:** `mvn -pl Shared -am test`
{{- end}}

## Stage 5 — EventConsumer
- Add `case OrderShipped` to the listener `switch (event)` block
- Idempotency: call `idempotencyTracker.checkAndMark(event.eventId())` first
- Default arm `throw` is preserved (sealed-switch invariant)
**Validate:** `mvn -pl EventConsumer -am verify`
**Depends on:** Stage 2 (OrderShipped permits update)

## Final verification
- [ ] `mvn install`
- [ ] Publish an OrderShipped event in tests; assert listener fires
- [ ] DLT/DLQ wiring exercises happy path
```
{{- else}}

(This project does not include the EventConsumer module — event
features are not applicable. Add via `trabuco add EventConsumer` if
you need event-driven processing.)
{{- end}}

### Archetype 4 — Rename / cross-cutting refactor (NOT stratified)

Renames must be **atomic** across all modules. Stratified ordering would
leave intermediate stages that don't compile (Stage 1 renames in Model;
Stages 2-5 still reference the old name).

```markdown
# Plan: rename Order → PurchaseOrder across the project

## Single atomic stage
- Update Order.java → PurchaseOrder.java (and all related files: Record,
  Repository, Service, Controller, Tests)
- Update all imports and type references in one pass
- Update @Table("orders") → @Table("purchase_orders") in record
- Add migration: `V<N>__rename_orders_to_purchase_orders.sql`
**Validate:** `mvn install` after the entire rename completes

**Plan archetype:** atomic, not stratified — partial states would not compile.
```

For renames that affect a database table, the migration is part of the
same atomic change but applied at runtime by Flyway after deploy.

### Archetype 5 — Bug fix (dual direction)

Bugs travel top-down through the call stack but must be fixed bottom-up
to keep stages buildable. The plan should show both directions.

```markdown
# Plan: fix 500 on GET /api/orders/{id} — root cause: missing repository method

## Diagnosis (top-down)
1. `OrderController.getById` returns 500 on missing IDs
2. `OrderService.findOrFail` throws `NoSuchElementException`
3. `OrderRepository` has `findById` but no `findOrFail` helper
4. Root cause: missing `findOrFail` repository method

## Fix (bottom-up)
{{- if .HasModule "SQLDatastore"}}

### Stage 1 — SQLDatastore (add helper, fix root cause)
- Add `default OrderRecord findOrFail(Long id)` calling `findById`/orElseThrow
**Validate:** `mvn -pl SQLDatastore -am test`
{{- end}}

{{- if .HasModule "Shared"}}

### Stage 2 — Shared (use the helper)
- Update `OrderService.findOrFail` to call `repository.findOrFail`
**Validate:** `mvn -pl Shared -am test`
{{- end}}

{{- if .HasModule "API"}}

### Stage 3 — API (verify the fix surfaces correctly)
- Add `@ExceptionHandler(NoSuchElementException.class)` returning 404
- Add IT covering both the 200 happy path and 404 not-found path
**Validate:** `mvn -pl API -am verify`
{{- end}}
```

### Archetype 6 — Single-module work

Refactors / small fixes that touch one module are one stage. Don't pad
the plan with empty stages.

```markdown
# Plan: extract validation in OrderService.create into private validate()

## Stage 1 — Shared (only stage)
- Move 3 validation blocks from create() into `private void validate(OrderRequest)`
- Update create() to call validate() at the top
**Validate:** `mvn -pl Shared -am test`
```

### Archetype 7 — Migration-only (Flyway)

Pure schema change with no Java code. One stage in `SQLDatastore`.
{{if .HasModule "SQLDatastore"}}

```markdown
# Plan: add index on orders.customer_id

## Stage 1 — SQLDatastore (only stage)
- Add `V<N>__add_index_on_customer_id.sql`:
  ```sql
  CREATE INDEX IF NOT EXISTS idx_orders_customer_id ON orders (customer_id);
  ```
**Validate:** `mvn -pl SQLDatastore -am test` (Flyway picks up the new V{N})
```
